<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
    <title>1.1. Evaluating line integrals</title>

    <p>
        <alert>MTH 401</alert>
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="subsubsec-vector-calculus">
        <title>Section 1: Vector Calculus</title>

        <subsubsection>
            <title>Part 1.1: Line Integrals</title>

            <introduction>
                <p>
                    The first portion of this class will look a little closer at
                    vector calculus.
                    In particular, here we will focus on line integrals.
                </p>
            </introduction>
        </subsubsection>
    </subsection>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Suppose that <m>{\mathbf F}(x,y) = (x^2, xy)</m> and that
                <m>C_1</m>, <m>C_2</m>, and <m>C_3</m> are curves as shown
                below.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="2.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate the line integrals
                    <md>
                        \lineint{C_1}{F}, ~~~
                        \lineint{C_2}{F}, ~~~
                        \lineint{C_3}{F}.
                    </md>
                </p>

                <image width="35%" margins="0% 65%">
                    <shortdescription>
                        The graph of three line segments with directions.
                    </shortdescription>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-line-integral">
                        <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,4,4)">
                                <grid-axes spacings="((-1,1,4),(-1,1,4))" xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
                                <vector v="(2,0)" tail="(1,1)" stroke="blue" head-location="0.8"/>
                                <vector v="(0,2)" tail="(3,1)" stroke="blue" head-location="0.8"/>
                                <vector v="(2,2)" tail="(1,1)" stroke="blue" head-location="0.7"/>
                                <label anchor="(2,1)" alignment="south" offset="(0,-5)">
                                    <m>
                                        \displaystyle C_1
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <label anchor="(3,2)" alignment="east" offset="(5,0)">
                                    <m>
                                        \displaystyle C_2
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                                <label anchor="(2,2)" alignment="northwest" offset="(2,2)">
                                    <m>
                                        \displaystyle C_3
                                    </m>
                                </label>
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>

                    <description>
                        <p>
                            Three directional curves are shown, <m>C_1</m> is
                            the straight line from <m>(1,1)</m> to <m>(3,1)</m>,
                            <m>C_2</m> is the line from <m>(3,1)</m> to
                            <m>(3,3)</m>, and <m>C_3</m> is the line from
                            <m>(1,1)</m> to <m>(3,3)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </description>
                </image>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                <m>C_1</m>: parameterization <m>x=x, y=1, 1\le x\le 3</m>. So 
                <m>dx = dx, dy=0</m>. 
                <md>
                  \lineint{C_1}{F} = \int_1^3 x^2 dx + x(1)(0)=\left.\frac13 x^3\right|_1^3 = 9-\frac13=\frac{26}{3}
                </md>
                
              </p>
              <p>
                <m>C_2</m>: parameterization <m>x=3, y=y, 1\le y\le 3</m>. So 
                <m>dx = 0, dy=dy</m>. 
                <md>
                  \lineint{C_2}{F} = \int_1^3 9(0) + 3ydy=\left.\frac32 y^2\right|_1^3 = \frac32(9-1)=12
                </md>
                
              </p>
              <p>
                <m>C_3</m>: parameterization <m>x=x, y=x, 1\le y\le 3</m>. So 
                <m>dx = dx, dy=dy</m>. 
                <md>
                  \lineint{C_3}{F} = \int_1^3 x^2dx + x^2dx=\left.\frac23 x^3\right|_1^3 = \frac23(27-1)=\frac{52}{3}
                </md>
                
              </p>
              <p>
                Could also have parameterized with <m>t</m> or another way!
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find <m>\lineint{C}{F}</m> if <m>C</m> is the curve
                    traversing the triangle in the counter-clockwise direction.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                <m>\lineint{C}{F} = \lineint{C_1}{F}+\lineint{C_2}{F}-\lineint{C_3}{F}</m>
              </p>
              <p>
                <m>=\frac{26}{3} + 12 - \frac{52}{3} = \frac{10}{3}</m>
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Notice that the combined path of <m>C_1+C_2</m> starts and
                    stops at the same points as the path of <m>C_3</m>.
                    Does <m>\lineint{C_1+C_2}{F} = \lineint{C_3}{F}</m>?
                    That is, does the path matter or are all line integrals
                    independent of path?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                No they are not the same.  The path seems to matter here.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the field <m>{\mathbf F}(x,y) = (x,y)</m> and suppose
                that <m>C_1</m> is the upper half of the unit circle traversed
                in the counter-clockwise direction starting at <m>(1,0)</m> and
                ending at <m>(-1,0)</m>.
                Suppose that <m>C_2</m> is the straight line segment beginning
                at <m>(1,0)</m> and ending at <m>(-1,0)</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="3.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the line integrals:
                    <md>
                        \lineint{C_1}{F}, ~~~
  \lineint{C_2}{F}.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                <m>C_1</m>:  Parameterization <m>x = \cos(t), y=\sin(t)</m> (or <m>\vec{r}(t)=(\cos(t),\sin(t))</m>), with
                <m>0\le t \le \pi</m>.  Then <m>dx = -\sin(t)dt, dy=\cos(t)dt</m> (or <m>d\vec{r}(t)=(-\sin(t)dt,\cos(t)dt)</m>), so 
                the integral becomes <md>\lineint{C_1}{F}=\int_{C_1}xdx + ydy = \int_0^{\pi} \cos(t)(-\sin t) + \sin t(\cos t) dt =0</md>
              </p>
              <p>
                <m>C_2</m>:  Parameterization <m>x = x, y=0</m> (or <m>\vec{r}(x)=(x,0)</m>), with
                <m>x \rightarrow 1 \rightarrow -1</m>.  Then <m>dx = dx, dy=0</m> (or <m>d\vec{r}(x)=(dx,0)</m>), so 
                the integral becomes <md>\lineint{C_2}{F}=\int_{C_2}xdx + ydy = \int_1^{-1} xdx+0= \left.\frac12 x^2 \right|_1^{-1} = \frac12(1-1) =0</md>
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Give a geometric explanation for the line integral along
                    <m>C_1</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                On the unit circle (the path along <m>C_1</m>), <m>d\vec{r}</m> points tangent to the unit circle, and 
                <m>\vec{F}</m> points outward, orthogonal to the <m>d\vec{r}</m>.  So <m>\vec{F}</m> and <m>d\vec{r}</m>
                have a dot product of 0 along the whole curve, so the sum is also naturally zero.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the line integral <m>\lineint{C}{F}</m> where <m>C</m>
                    is the contour that begins at <m>(1,0)</m>, traverses the
                    upper half of the unit circle to <m>(-1,0)</m> and then
                    returns to <m>(1,0)</m> along the <m>x</m>-axis.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                <m>\lineint{C}{F} = \lineint{C_1}{F} - \lineint{C_2}{F} = 0-0=0</m>
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
        
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Consider the field <m>{\mathbf F}(x,y) = (y^2, 2xy+3)</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="3.0in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Evaluate the line integrals
                    <md>
                        \lineint{C_1+C_2}{F}, ~~~
  \lineint{C_3}{F},
                    </md>
                    where <m>C_1</m>, <m>C_2</m>, and <m>C_3</m> are the curves
                    from the first problem.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                <m>C_1</m>: parameterization <m>x=x, y=1, 1\le x\le 3</m>. So 
                <m>dx = dx, dy=0</m>. 
                <md>
                  \lineint{C_1}{F} = \int_{C_1} y^2 dx + (2xy+3)dy=
                  \int_1^3 1 dx + 0=\left.x\right|_1^3 = 2
                </md>
                
              </p>
              <p>
                <m>C_2</m>: parameterization <m>x=3, y=y, 1\le y\le 3</m>. So 
                <m>dx = 0, dy=dy</m>. 
                <md>
                  \lineint{C_2}{F} = \int_1^3 0 + 2*3*y+3dy=\left.(3 y^2 + 3y)\right|_1^3 = 30
                </md>
                
              </p>
              <p>
                Thus <md>
                  \lineint{C_1+C_2}{F} = \lineint{C_1}{F}+\lineint{C_2}{F} = 2 + 30 = 32.
                </md>
                
              </p>
              <p>
                <m>C_3</m>: parameterization <m>x=x, y=x, 1\le y\le 3</m>. So 
                <m>dx = dx, dy=dy</m>. 
                <md>
                  \lineint{C_3}{F} = \int_1^3 x^2+2x^2+3dx= \left.(x^3+3x)\right|_1^3 = 32
                </md>
                
              </p>
              <p>
                They're the same... interesting.  Could also have parameterized with <m>t</m> or another way!
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is it possible to write <m>{\mathbf F} = \nabla f</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="show-soln">
              <p>
                If so, then <m>\mathbf F = (\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}, \frac{\partial f}{\partial y}) = (y^2, 2xy+3)</m>.
                One possibility seems to be <md>f = xy^2+3y</md>.  (Could add any constant.)
              </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose that
                <md>
                    {\mathbf F}(x,y) = \left(-\frac{y}{x^2+y^2},
    \frac{x}{x^2+y^2}\right)
                </md>
                and <m>C</m> is the unit circle traversed one time in the
                counter-clockwise direction.
                Find the line integral <m>\lineint{C}{F}</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution component="show-soln">
          <p>
            Parameterization <md>x = \cos(t), y=\sin(t)</md> (or <m>\vec{r}(t)=(\cos(t),\sin(t))</m>), with
                <m>0\le t \le 2\pi</m>.  Then <m>dx = -\sin(t)dt, dy=\cos(t)dt</m> (or <m>d\vec{r}(t)=(-\sin(t)dt,\cos(t)dt)</m>), 
                but also <md>x^2+y^2 = \cos^2(t)+\sin^2(t)=1</md> so 
                the integral becomes <md>\lineint{C}{F}=\int_{C}\frac{-y}{x^2+y^2}dx + \frac{x}{x^2+y^2}dy </md>
                <md>=
                   \int_0^{2\pi} -\sin t(-\sin t) + \cos t(\cos t) dt 
                </md><md>= \int_0^{2\pi} \sin^2(t) + \cos^2(t) dt
                </md><md>  = \int_0^{2\pi} 1 dt</md><md> = 2\pi</md>
          </p>
          <p>
            Notice it is not zero.  This is an important example.  We'll come back to this.
          </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>